## Original Content
And when is each system used? Here’s what you need to know about writing in Japanese.

## Summary
This article explains why Japanese employs three distinct writing systems and clarifies the specific contexts in which each system is used. It provides an educational overview to help readers understand the application of hiragana, katakana, and kanji in writing Japanese.

## Key Points
- Japanese has three writing systems: hiragana, katakana, and kanji, each serving different purposes.
- Hiragana is used for native Japanese words and grammatical elements.
- Katakana is primarily for foreign words, onomatopoeia, and emphasis.
- Kanji represents Chinese characters used for meanings and roots of words.
- Understanding these systems is crucial for accurate reading and writing in Japanese.


